SAT_CHUNK("rw/words-in-context/3/0",[
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.37f5e8260c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-23","passage":"<p>Early reviews of the museum's renovation were ______. Critics praised the brighter galleries and the new wing, but many complained that the redesigned entrance was confusing and that several beloved works had been moved to storage.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["effusive","mixed","belated","scathing"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The reviews included both praise (brighter galleries) and complaints (the confusing entrance), so they were \"mixed.\" \"Effusive\" means gushing praise, which ignores the complaints. \"Scathing\" means harshly critical, which ignores the praise. \"Belated\" means late, and nothing is said about timing."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.fa5a457fa6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-24","passage":"<p>Although the senator's speech was widely described as a sharp attack on the proposed bill, a close reading of the transcript reveals a more ______ position: she criticized two provisions but explicitly endorsed the bill's overall goals and said she might vote for an amended version.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["ambiguous","hostile","conventional","nuanced"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The transcript shows she criticized parts but supported the goals and might vote yes: a position with fine distinctions, or \"nuanced.\" \"Hostile\" matches the misreading the sentence corrects. \"Ambiguous\" (unclear) is tempting, but her position was clearly stated, just more complex than reported. \"Conventional\" has no support."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.8e0acddca8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-25","passage":"<p>The new policy has been in effect for only six months, so its effects on graduation rates remain ______. School officials say that at least two more years of data will be needed before any conclusions can be drawn.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["controversial","indeterminate","negligible","detrimental"],"answer":"B","explanation":"With too little data to draw conclusions, the effects can't yet be determined: \"indeterminate.\" \"Negligible\" (tiny) is a conclusion the officials say they can't reach yet. \"Detrimental\" is also a conclusion. \"Controversial\" suggests dispute, which the text doesn't mention."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.7000bfd783","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-26","passage":"<p>Marine biologist Rachel Tanaka's findings on coral bleaching have proved remarkably ______: laboratories in Australia, Hawaii, and the Red Sea, using different methods and coral species, have all obtained similar results.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["robust","preliminary","idiosyncratic","provocative"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Results that hold up across different labs, methods, and species are \"robust.\" \"Provocative\" means stirring controversy, and \"idiosyncratic\" means peculiar to one case, which contradicts the repeated confirmations. \"Preliminary\" means early and not yet confirmed, but these results have been confirmed."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.62caeee769","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-27","passage":"<p>The author's prose is so ______ that a single paragraph can require several readings: sentences stretch across half a page, folding clause within clause, and ordinary objects are described in terms borrowed from law, astronomy, and medieval theology.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["whimsical","terse","lucid","dense"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Long, layered sentences full of specialized references make the prose \"dense,\" which is why readers must reread. \"Lucid\" (clear) and \"terse\" (brief) are the opposites. \"Whimsical\" means playful, and nothing suggests playfulness."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.39822924a9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-28","passage":"<p>Historians once treated the letters of Civil War soldiers as ______ accounts of battle, but many soldiers wrote with their families in mind and softened or omitted details that might cause alarm at home.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["unvarnished","sentimental","incomplete","fabricated"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The contrast (\"but... softened or omitted details\") shows historians had thought the letters were plain, unfiltered accounts: \"unvarnished.\" \"Incomplete\" describes what the letters actually turned out to be, not what historians once thought. \"Sentimental\" doesn't fit the earlier view, and \"fabricated\" overstates it, since softening details isn't inventing them."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.37edcb374b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-29","passage":"<p>The mayor's plan to reduce traffic has been criticized from two ______ directions: cycling advocates argue that it does too little to discourage driving, while business owners contend that it will drive customers away by making parking harder to find.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["legitimate","unexpected","opposing","similar"],"answer":"C","explanation":"One group says the plan does too little and the other says it goes too far, so the criticisms come from \"opposing\" directions. \"Similar\" contradicts this. \"Unexpected\" and \"legitimate\" aren't supported, since the sentence doesn't say whether the criticism was surprising or valid."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.53b06f7683","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-30","passage":"<p>Because the documents had been ______ over several centuries by scribes who corrected what they saw as errors, scholars trying to recover the original text must compare dozens of copies to identify later changes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["preserved","altered","translated","discovered"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Scribes \"corrected\" the documents, which means they changed them, so scholars must find the changes: \"altered.\" \"Preserved\" suggests keeping them unchanged, the opposite of what happened. Nothing mentions translation into another language, and \"discovered over several centuries\" doesn't explain the need to find later changes."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.ce0b699c91","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-31","passage":"<p>Unlike her predecessor, who announced major decisions only after they had been finalized, the new director has made a point of ______ staff members early, inviting them to comment on proposals while they are still being drafted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["consulting","rewarding","informing","evaluating"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Inviting comments on drafts means asking for input: \"consulting.\" \"Informing\" (telling) is closer to the predecessor's approach and doesn't involve asking staff for their views. \"Rewarding\" and \"evaluating\" don't match inviting comments."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.b1f576ad4f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-32","passage":"<p>The scientist's claim that the fossil represents a new species has been met with ______ by many colleagues, who note that the specimen is incomplete and could belong to a juvenile of a species already known.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["enthusiasm","hostility","indifference","skepticism"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The colleagues raise reasons to doubt the claim, which is \"skepticism.\" \"Hostility\" suggests anger or aggression beyond measured doubt. \"Indifference\" means not caring, yet the colleagues respond with specific objections. \"Enthusiasm\" is the opposite."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.c4ab9e9641","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-33","passage":"<p>Although the two studies reached similar conclusions, their methods were ______: one surveyed thousands of households by phone, while the other followed twelve families through daily observation for a year.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["equivalent","rigorous","flawed","disparate"],"answer":"D","explanation":"A large phone survey and a year of observing twelve families are very different methods: \"disparate.\" \"Equivalent\" says the opposite. \"Flawed\" and \"rigorous\" judge quality, but the sentence contrasts the methods without judging them."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.ae6bf4a263","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-34","passage":"<p>The committee's report was careful not to ______ blame for the bridge's collapse on any single agency, noting that design flaws, delayed inspections, and budget cuts had each played a role.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["conceal","deflect","accept","assign"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The report spreads the causes across several factors, so it avoids putting blame on one agency: \"assign blame.\" \"Deflect blame\" means pushing it away from oneself, which doesn't fit a report about other agencies. The committee isn't at fault, so \"accept\" doesn't fit, and \"conceal blame\" isn't a meaningful phrase here."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.47453bc999","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-35","passage":"<p>The explorer's journals, long thought lost, turned out to be ______: the entries for the expedition's final month, which might have explained its failure, had been torn out before the journals were donated to the library.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["voluminous","illegible","apocryphal","fragmentary"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Missing pages make the journals incomplete, or \"fragmentary.\" \"Illegible\" means unreadable, but the problem is missing pages, not handwriting. \"Apocryphal\" means of doubtful authenticity, which isn't suggested. \"Voluminous\" (extensive) contradicts the missing entries."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.e624949230","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-36","passage":"<p>In her memoir, the chef describes her early training as ______: for two years she was permitted only to chop onions and wash pots, and she was not allowed to cook a single dish until her third year in the kitchen.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["negligible","haphazard","grueling","lavish"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Two years of chopping and washing before cooking anything describes demanding, exhausting training: \"grueling.\" \"Haphazard\" means disorganized, but this training followed a strict, deliberate order. \"Lavish\" (generous) and \"negligible\" (tiny) don't fit."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.5d19f5150b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-37","passage":"<p>The candidate's answer was so ______ that both supporters and opponents of the tax proposal left the debate believing she agreed with them.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["equivocal","candid","impassioned","succinct"],"answer":"A","explanation":"If both sides think she agreed with them, her answer could be read either way: \"equivocal.\" A \"candid\" (frank) answer would have made her position clear. \"Impassioned\" (emotional) and \"succinct\" (brief) don't explain why both sides heard agreement."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.7b9291063f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-38","passage":"<p>Though often portrayed as a solitary genius, the inventor relied heavily on a team of assistants, and recent scholarship has sought to ______ their contributions, which the inventor's own accounts rarely mentioned.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["recover","replicate","exaggerate","diminish"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The assistants' work was left out of the inventor's accounts, so scholars are trying to bring it back to light: \"recover.\" \"Diminish\" would repeat the neglect. \"Exaggerate\" implies overstating, which the text doesn't suggest. \"Replicate\" means repeat the work, not restore recognition."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.e8fd93b240","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-39","passage":"<p>The drought's effects on the region's farms were ______ rather than immediate: yields fell only slightly in the first year, but as groundwater reserves shrank, losses grew larger each season.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["temporary","cumulative","negligible","widespread"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Losses that build season after season are \"cumulative,\" which contrasts with \"immediate.\" \"Negligible\" ignores the growing losses. \"Temporary\" contradicts losses that keep increasing. \"Widespread\" concerns area, not how effects build over time."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.95b94328f1","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-40","passage":"<p>The novelist is known for endings that ______ easy resolution: her books often close with characters facing the same choices they faced at the start, their futures left deliberately uncertain.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["demand","provide","resist","anticipate"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Endings that leave futures uncertain avoid tidy resolution: they \"resist\" it. \"Demand\" and \"provide\" suggest the opposite. \"Anticipate\" means expect, which doesn't fit endings that withhold resolution."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.bbe49c7557","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-41","passage":"<p>The survey's findings should be interpreted ______, since respondents were volunteers who may have been more interested in the topic than the general public.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["immediately","cautiously","enthusiastically","literally"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Volunteers may not represent the public, so the findings deserve care: \"cautiously.\" \"Enthusiastically\" ignores the limitation. \"Literally\" and \"immediately\" don't address the sampling concern."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.e48fb85878","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-42","passage":"<p>Rather than ______ the long-standing theory outright, the researchers proposed a modification: the theory's predictions held for large animals, they argued, but required adjustment for species weighing less than a kilogram.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["revising","discarding","testing","defending"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The researchers chose modification over complete rejection, so the blank must mean rejecting the theory: \"discarding.\" \"Revising\" is what they actually did, so it can't be the alternative they chose against. \"Defending\" and \"testing\" don't contrast with modification."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.b836731bcf","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data3-43","passage":"<p>The committee found the evidence of wrongdoing ______: although several witnesses had described irregular payments, no documents confirmed their accounts, and two witnesses later withdrew their statements.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["incriminating","conclusive","abundant","tenuous"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Unconfirmed testimony, some of it withdrawn, is weak evidence: \"tenuous.\" \"Conclusive\" and \"abundant\" are opposites. \"Incriminating\" (suggesting guilt) doesn't capture the evidence's weakness, which is the point of the colon's explanation."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.df3e55bb1f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-0","passage":"<p>Reviewers of the biography have praised its research while questioning its tone. The author, they note, is so ______ toward her subject that she declines to examine even the well-documented failures of his final years.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["deferential","courteous","impartial","sympathetic"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The author avoids examining documented failures, which suggests excessive respect that prevents criticism: \"deferential.\" \"Courteous\" means polite in manner, which doesn't explain refusing to examine evidence. \"Sympathetic\" would suggest understanding of the subject's difficulties, but the issue is deference to him. \"Impartial\" means neutral, the opposite of the reviewers' complaint."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.944bd7fd97","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-1","passage":"<p>Early results from the trial were promising, but the research team's public statements remained ______: the sample was small, the follow-up period short, and several patients had left the study before it ended.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["indifferent","pessimistic","measured","evasive"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Given real limitations, the team spoke carefully and without overstating: \"measured.\" \"Pessimistic\" would mean expecting a bad outcome, but the results were promising. \"Evasive\" implies dodging questions, which the listed reasons contradict. \"Indifferent\" would mean they didn't care about the results."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.e56251967d","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-2","passage":"<p>The court's ruling did not overturn the earlier decision so much as ______ it: judges left the central holding intact while narrowing the circumstances in which it applies.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["reverse","clarify","endorse","qualify"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The judges kept the main holding but limited where it applies, which is to add limits: \"qualify.\" \"Endorse\" means approve without change. \"Clarify\" would mean making the meaning clearer rather than narrowing its reach. \"Reverse\" means overturn, which the sentence explicitly rules out."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.e4010b2fcc","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-3","passage":"<p>For a poet known for elaborate imagery, the late work is startlingly ______: a typical poem runs six lines, names one object, and stops.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["fragmentary","austere","somber","accessible"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Six lines, one object, no ornament describes a bare, stripped-down style: \"austere.\" \"Somber\" refers to a gloomy mood, not to brevity. \"Accessible\" means easy to understand, which shortness doesn't guarantee. \"Fragmentary\" means broken into incomplete pieces, but these poems are complete."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.2ed7c1b90a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-4","passage":"<p>Museum conservators were reluctant to clean the painting because the varnish, though yellowed, had proved ______ over two centuries, protecting the pigments beneath it from smoke and humidity.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["pristine","decorative","translucent","durable"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The varnish lasted two centuries and kept protecting the paint, so it has held up well: \"durable.\" \"Decorative\" concerns appearance, but the varnish is yellowed and valued for protection. \"Translucent\" describes how light passes through it, which doesn't explain the reluctance to remove it. \"Pristine\" means spotless, contradicting \"yellowed.\""},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.7036e4d755","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-5","passage":"<p>The mayor described the budget compromise as ______, and the description was apt: it satisfied no one completely, but every council member could point to one provision worth defending.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["decisive","workable","ideal","controversial"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A compromise that pleases nobody fully yet gives everyone something is one that functions in practice: \"workable.\" \"Ideal\" contradicts \"satisfied no one completely.\" \"Decisive\" suggests settling matters firmly, which a partial compromise doesn't. \"Controversial\" would fit a disputed plan, but the sentence emphasizes that each member found something to defend."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.0d18560df5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-6","passage":"<p>Historians disagree about the general's motives, in part because his memoir is so ______: he describes the weather on the morning of the battle in half a page and the decision to retreat in a single clause.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["uneven","disorganized","unreliable","concise"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Lavish detail on trivia and almost none on the key decision shows wildly inconsistent treatment: \"uneven.\" \"Unreliable\" would mean inaccurate, but the complaint is about proportion, not truth. \"Concise\" describes brevity throughout, contradicted by the half page on weather. \"Disorganized\" refers to poor arrangement, not to unbalanced emphasis."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.544748bfee","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-7","passage":"<p>Though the two economists reached opposite conclusions about the policy, their disagreement proved ______: each was forced to state assumptions that had gone unexamined, and both later revised their models.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["superficial","amicable","irreconcilable","productive"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The disagreement led to clearer assumptions and revised models, so it produced something useful: \"productive.\" \"Amicable\" means friendly, but nothing describes their manner. \"Superficial\" would mean shallow, contradicted by the deep revisions. \"Irreconcilable\" means it couldn't be resolved, yet both economists changed their models."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.5c2b6e43a0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-8","passage":"<p>The novel's dialogue is deliberately ______: characters interrupt one another, trail off, and repeat themselves in ways that a more polished script would smooth away.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["naturalistic","ornate","stilted","economical"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Interruptions, trailing off, and repetition imitate how people actually speak: \"naturalistic.\" \"Stilted\" means stiff and unnatural, the opposite. \"Economical\" means using few words, but repetition is the opposite of economy. \"Ornate\" means elaborately decorated."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.1ed3fdb2c5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-9","passage":"<p>The report's recommendations are ______ rather than prescriptive: it describes several approaches other cities have taken and leaves the choice among them to local officials.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["suggestive","ambiguous","tentative","exhaustive"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Offering options and leaving the decision to others is presenting possibilities rather than directing: \"suggestive.\" \"Ambiguous\" means unclear in meaning, but the approaches are described plainly. \"Tentative\" implies uncertainty about the ideas themselves. \"Exhaustive\" means covering everything, which \"several approaches\" does not."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.60695a4060","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-10","passage":"<p>Critics who call the director's style cold miss how carefully her films are ______: a single shot of an unmade bed can carry the weight that another filmmaker would give to a monologue.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["compressed","improvised","restrained","sentimental"],"answer":"A","explanation":"One brief shot carries what another director needs a whole speech for, which means much is packed into little: \"compressed.\" \"Restrained\" describes holding back emotion, which is closer to the \"cold\" charge than to the defense. \"Sentimental\" means excessively emotional. \"Improvised\" means unplanned, contradicting \"carefully.\""},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.40627e6b06","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-11","passage":"<p>The committee's report was ______ in its criticism of the agency: it named specific officials, cited dates, and quoted internal emails at length.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["pointed","reluctant","vague","harsh"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Naming officials, citing dates, and quoting emails is criticism aimed precisely at targets: \"pointed.\" \"Harsh\" describes severity of tone, which the details don't establish. \"Vague\" is the opposite of specific. \"Reluctant\" would suggest hesitancy, contradicted by the detail."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.080c256a8f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-12","passage":"<p>Far from being ______, the tradition of building with rammed earth has been revived by architects who value its low cost and its ability to moderate indoor temperatures.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["unfamiliar","obsolete","impractical","ancient"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The contrast (\"Far from being... has been revived\") requires a word meaning no longer in use: \"obsolete.\" \"Unfamiliar\" concerns whether people know of it, not whether it's used. \"Impractical\" is contradicted by low cost and temperature control, but it doesn't fit the revival contrast as directly as a word about falling out of use. \"Ancient\" isn't contradicted by a revival, since an old method can be revived."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.f8b058d461","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-13","passage":"<p>The lecture was billed as an introduction, but the speaker's use of unexplained notation made it ______ to everyone in the room except the graduate students.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["elementary","offensive","tedious","impenetrable"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Unexplained notation made the talk impossible to follow for non-specialists: \"impenetrable.\" \"Tedious\" means boring, which unexplained notation doesn't establish. \"Elementary\" means simple, contradicting the difficulty. \"Offensive\" doesn't fit a complaint about notation."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.5bd3f5eaed","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-14","passage":"<p>Rather than ______ the differences among the three accounts, the historian treats them as evidence that witnesses in the crowd saw genuinely different parts of the event.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["recording","reconciling","disputing","exaggerating"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The alternative to treating differences as real evidence is trying to make them agree: \"reconciling.\" \"Disputing\" means arguing against the accounts, not resolving their differences. \"Exaggerating\" means overstating, which isn't the alternative described. \"Recording\" means writing down, which doesn't contrast with treating the differences as evidence."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.7566417595","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-15","passage":"<p>The startup's early advantage proved ______: within two years, three larger companies had copied its main feature and bundled it into products customers already owned.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["illusory","modest","fleeting","decisive"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The advantage was real but disappeared quickly once rivals copied it: \"fleeting.\" \"Illusory\" means it never existed, but it did exist for two years. \"Modest\" describes size, not duration. \"Decisive\" means it settled the competition, which the copying contradicts."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.7a9226c6c4","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-16","passage":"<p>Because the samples had been stored improperly for a decade, the team treated its own measurements as ______, useful for designing the next study but not for drawing conclusions.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["provisional","confidential","erroneous","comprehensive"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Results good enough to guide the next study but not to conclude from are temporary and subject to revision: \"provisional.\" \"Erroneous\" would mean flatly wrong, yet the team still found them useful. \"Comprehensive\" means complete. \"Confidential\" concerns secrecy."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.c88b4880f9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-17","passage":"<p>The essayist's argument is ______ enough to survive the loss of any single example: remove the anecdote about the fishing fleet and the case still stands on the census data and the shipping records.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["familiar","cautious","robust","intricate"],"answer":"C","explanation":"An argument that holds up even when part is removed is sturdy: \"robust.\" \"Intricate\" describes complexity, not resilience. \"Familiar\" means well known. \"Cautious\" describes a careful manner of arguing, not the argument's strength."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.1fed4b2f34","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-18","passage":"<p>Museum labels once described the mask as the work of an anonymous craftsman; recent scholarship has ______ it to a workshop whose other pieces share the same distinctive incising.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["donated","compared","restored","attributed"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Assigning the mask to a particular workshop on the basis of shared technique is crediting authorship: \"attributed.\" \"Restored\" means repaired. \"Donated\" means given away. \"Compared\" would note similarities without assigning the work to the workshop."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.0f448ebef2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-19","passage":"<p>The coach's praise was ______: he singled out the defense's positioning in the second half rather than congratulating the team in general terms.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["premature","effusive","grudging","specific"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Singling out one aspect instead of speaking generally is praise aimed at particulars: \"specific.\" \"Effusive\" means gushing, which the contrast with \"general terms\" doesn't establish. \"Grudging\" means given unwillingly. \"Premature\" means too early, and nothing suggests bad timing."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.8823fa89bc","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-20","passage":"<p>The two treatments appear similar on paper, but in clinical use the newer one is ______: it requires a single injection rather than a daily pill regimen that many patients abandon.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["obsolete","identical","costlier","preferable"],"answer":"D","explanation":"A single injection instead of a regimen patients quit makes the newer treatment better in practice: \"preferable.\" \"Identical\" contradicts the difference described. \"Costlier\" isn't supported, since no prices are mentioned. \"Obsolete\" would mean outdated, but the newer treatment is the improvement."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.4c5256db3c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-21","passage":"<p>Although the manuscript was written in the 1860s, its account of the flood is ______: the author was a child at the time and composed the passage nearly forty years later, drawing on what neighbors had told her.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["contemporaneous","exaggerated","secondhand","fictional"],"answer":"C","explanation":"An account written decades later from what others said is not firsthand: \"secondhand.\" \"Fictional\" would mean invented, which the text doesn't claim. \"Contemporaneous\" means written at the same time as the event, the opposite of what's described. \"Exaggerated\" would mean overstated, which the explanation doesn't address."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.0cac85cfbe","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-22","passage":"<p>What makes the discovery significant is not the fossil itself, which is ______, but its location: no member of the group had previously been found so far north.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["damaged","unremarkable","valuable","ancient"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The significance comes from location \"not the fossil itself,\" so the fossil is ordinary: \"unremarkable.\" \"Damaged\" would describe its condition, which isn't mentioned. \"Ancient\" would apply to any fossil and wouldn't contrast with significance. \"Valuable\" contradicts the idea that the fossil itself isn't what matters."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.d6a90f1ab0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-23","passage":"<p>The new regulations are ______ in scope: they apply only to facilities that process more than 50,000 tons annually, which excludes the great majority of plants.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["vague","narrow","experimental","stringent"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Applying only to the largest facilities and excluding most plants makes the scope limited: \"narrow.\" \"Vague\" concerns clarity, but the threshold is precise. \"Stringent\" means strict, which describes how demanding the rules are, not how many plants they cover. \"Experimental\" would mean a trial."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.ac5af192b1","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-24","passage":"<p>Readers who expect a defense of the policy will find the book ______: the author spends most of it explaining how the policy came to seem inevitable, not whether it was wise.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["derivative","disorienting","comprehensive","persuasive"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A reader expecting one thing and finding another is thrown off: \"disorienting.\" \"Persuasive\" would mean convinced by an argument the book doesn't make. \"Derivative\" means unoriginal, which isn't suggested. \"Comprehensive\" means complete, but the book leaves out the question of wisdom."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.615206743a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-25","passage":"<p>The trainer's method is ______ rather than corrective: instead of punishing unwanted behavior, she rewards the small movements that lead toward the behavior she wants.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["permissive","conventional","theoretical","incremental"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Rewarding small movements that build toward a goal proceeds step by step: \"incremental.\" \"Permissive\" means allowing anything, but she is shaping behavior deliberately. \"Theoretical\" contrasts with practical, not with corrective. \"Conventional\" means usual, and the sentence presents her method as an alternative."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.0cff2b9334","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-26","passage":"<p>The letters reveal a friendship more ______ than either writer's published work suggests: they argue about money, apologize, and pick the argument up again months later.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["formal","collaborative","enduring","turbulent"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Recurring arguments and apologies describe a stormy relationship: \"turbulent.\" \"Formal\" contradicts arguing about money and apologizing. \"Enduring\" concerns length, which the passage doesn't contrast with published work. \"Collaborative\" would describe working together."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.f02b61ac82","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-27","passage":"<p>Engineers describe the design as ______: the same panel serves as a wall in one configuration, a table in another, and a door in a third.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["minimal","provisional","redundant","versatile"],"answer":"D","explanation":"One panel serving three purposes is adaptable to many uses: \"versatile.\" \"Redundant\" means unnecessarily repeated. \"Minimal\" refers to using very little, which multiple functions don't establish. \"Provisional\" means temporary."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.89a8ec9068","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-28","passage":"<p>The review was ______ in a way that few readers noticed: every sentence praised the novel, yet the praise was reserved entirely for its cover design, its typeface, and its heft.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["hostile","inaccurate","exuberant","backhanded"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Praise that avoids the writing itself insults while appearing to compliment: \"backhanded.\" \"Inaccurate\" would mean factually wrong, but the praise is accurate as far as it goes. \"Hostile\" would be openly aggressive, yet every sentence is praise. \"Exuberant\" means enthusiastic and misses the concealed criticism."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.3bb5e2b88c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-29","passage":"<p>Because so few specimens survive, claims about the species' diet remain ______: researchers can describe what a single individual ate on one day but not what the population typically consumed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["conjectural","trivial","contested","outdated"],"answer":"A","explanation":"With evidence from one individual, broader claims rest on inference rather than proof: \"conjectural.\" \"Outdated\" would mean superseded by newer work. \"Trivial\" means unimportant, but diet claims matter. \"Contested\" would mean researchers dispute them, which the passage doesn't say."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.aca4c24b7c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-30","passage":"<p>Her influence on the field has been ______: she published only three papers, but each redirected a line of research that dozens of laboratories were pursuing.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["disproportionate","overstated","gradual","negligible"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Three papers producing effects far beyond their number is influence out of proportion to output: \"disproportionate.\" \"Negligible\" contradicts redirecting whole research lines. \"Gradual\" concerns pace, not scale. \"Overstated\" would mean her influence has been exaggerated, but the sentence documents it."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.c67a5a89de","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-31","passage":"<p>The building's façade is ______ of the neighborhood's industrial past: the brick pattern copies that of the textile mill that stood on the site until 1974.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["evocative","independent","dismissive","typical"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A façade that calls the old mill to mind brings the past vividly to mind: \"evocative.\" \"Typical\" would mean it resembles other buildings generally, not that it recalls a specific history. \"Dismissive\" means rejecting. \"Independent\" means unconnected, the opposite of copying the mill."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.0321200523","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-32","passage":"<p>The agency's response to the outbreak was ______: inspectors arrived within hours, and the contaminated lots were traced and recalled before the weekend.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["exhaustive","prompt","belated","adequate"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Inspectors within hours and recalls within days describe speed: \"prompt.\" \"Adequate\" means merely sufficient and understates the speed described. \"Exhaustive\" describes thoroughness, not timing. \"Belated\" means late, the opposite."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.29b7892fe4","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-33","passage":"<p>What the study measures is ______: participants reported how often they exercised, and self-reports of exercise are known to exceed actual activity by a wide margin.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["irrelevant","imprecise","unethical","novel"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Self-reports that overstate activity make the measurement inaccurate: \"imprecise.\" \"Irrelevant\" would mean exercise doesn't matter to the study, which isn't the issue. \"Unethical\" concerns conduct. \"Novel\" means new."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.59aa5ea5af","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-34","passage":"<p>The philosopher's prose is ______ without being obscure: sentences accumulate qualifications, but each qualification does work, and a patient reader is never lost.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["casual","repetitive","dense","pedantic"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Sentences packed with qualifications that all do work are thick with content yet followable: \"dense.\" \"Casual\" contradicts accumulated qualifications. \"Repetitive\" is ruled out because each qualification does its own work. \"Pedantic\" implies fussing over trivia, but the qualifications matter."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.fe0de254dc","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-35","passage":"<p>Opponents called the plan ______, and its own authors conceded that it depended on three separate technologies, none of which had been demonstrated at scale.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["expensive","unpopular","modest","speculative"],"answer":"D","explanation":"A plan resting on undemonstrated technologies is based on guesswork about what might work: \"speculative.\" \"Unpopular\" concerns public opinion, not the authors' concession. \"Expensive\" isn't supported, since no costs are given. \"Modest\" contradicts a plan requiring three new technologies."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.c12e977b9b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-36","passage":"<p>The editor's changes were ______: she moved two paragraphs, cut a subordinate clause, and left the argument, the evidence, and the conclusion untouched.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["necessary","cosmetic","careless","extensive"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Moving paragraphs and cutting a clause while leaving the substance alone is surface-level work: \"cosmetic.\" \"Extensive\" contradicts how little changed. \"Careless\" would imply errors, but the changes are deliberate. \"Necessary\" isn't established by the passage."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.e593ee786d","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-37","passage":"<p>Accounts of the expedition are ______ on the question of who first reached the summit: two members say they climbed together, a third insists he was alone, and the guides kept no record.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["silent","speculative","contradictory","unanimous"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Conflicting claims from participants make the accounts disagree: \"contradictory.\" \"Silent\" is ruled out because the members do speak about it. \"Unanimous\" means all agree, the opposite. \"Speculative\" would mean guessing, but these are firsthand claims."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.44a075a74a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-38","passage":"<p>The soil study's conclusions were ______ by later work: researchers repeating the measurements in four other valleys found the same relationship between fungal networks and seedling survival.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["questioned","superseded","corroborated","complicated"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Repeating the measurements elsewhere and finding the same relationship supports the original conclusions with new evidence: \"corroborated.\" \"Complicated,\" \"superseded,\" and \"questioned\" all suggest the later work weakened or replaced the conclusions, which matching results do not."},
{"id":"g.rw.words-in-context.3.9eb66a626f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-WC","group":"hard-hard_data7-39","passage":"<p>For a first novel, the book is remarkably ______: the author knows exactly how much to leave out, and the ending arrives three pages before a less experienced writer would have stopped.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["ambitious","derivative","polished","disciplined"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Knowing what to leave out and stopping early shows firm control over the material: \"disciplined.\" \"Ambitious\" would describe scope or aims, not restraint. \"Derivative\" means imitative. \"Polished\" refers to smooth finish, which doesn't capture the deliberate restraint described."}
]);
